        "body": "Enabled video-analysis rules can trigger alarms for qualifying events such as line crossing, loitering, falls, unauthorized access, crowding, or unusual behavior. The alarm provides a record for operator investigation. Available detections, indicators, and response states depend on the deployed features and configuration. An alarm turns a qualifying camera observation into work that an operator needs to assess. Begin by establishing the source and visible conditions, then determine whether the event requires action. The response record should show what was observed, what was checked, and which outcome was saved. Use live video for the current scene and the associated clip for the context around the trigger. A newly created clip may still be processing, so record what is known and return to the footage when it becomes available. Keep the observation time and camera reference consistent throughout the investigation. StageInformation or ActionLive AlertFind the affected camera through indicators such as a red border, highlighting, flashing, or an alarm label.Notification RecordReview the alarm type, time, camera, location, severity, and status. Filter records using the available pending, severe, non-severe, or confirmed criteria.Associated FootageReview footage around the trigger to establish what happened, how long it lasted, and whether intervention is needed. A new alarm clip may still be processing.Alarm DetailsReview AI detections, adjust the event classification, update the status, and add notes. Confirm, close, or mark a false alarm using the actions available in the deployment.Status UpdatesUse the updated event record, notification display, and statistics to track follow-up work and review the response. Treat detection, presentation, and response as related stages with separate checks. A configured rule must observe qualifying activity; the event must be available to the appropriate operator; the operator must verify the evidence and save an allowed outcome. During implementation, retain one event reference through those stages so a failure can be isolated without repeating unrelated configuration changes."
