    "contentHtml": "<h2>Overview</h2><p>Notification Center collects events across cameras, locations, and times. During monitoring, review recent alarms. For a historical investigation, set the date, time, and camera scope before refining the event type or status.</p><p>Use the notification to locate the event. Review its full details and footage before deciding on the outcome.</p><h2>Features</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Feature or Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>List Fields</th><td>Review the camera ID, date and time, location, event type, status, notes, and details action. Interpret severity labels separately from response states.</td></tr><tr><th>Keyword Search</th><td>Find records using supported fields such as camera ID, camera name, location, type, or notes.</td></tr><tr><th>Filter Panel</th><td>Combine dates, times, states, and event types, then apply the filters. An overly narrow scope can exclude the event you need.</td></tr><tr><th>Details Action</th><td>Select View to check the event, camera, detection results, clip, and response state.</td></tr><tr><th>Notes</th><td>Read the investigation context, issue description, and response basis recorded for the event.</td></tr><tr><th>Pagination</th><td>Use the page controls to browse more records within the current query.</td></tr><tr><th>Auto Refresh</th><td>Update the list at the configured interval during monitoring. Turn it off when you need a stable view of historical records.</td></tr><tr><th>Status Updates</th><td>Changes saved in the details update the list and related views. Verify the displayed result after completing the review.</td></tr></tbody></table><p data-editorial-revision=\"enterprise-v1.5\">Choose Auto Refresh according to the task. During active monitoring it helps expose recent activity; during a historical review, a stable query makes it easier to compare records and retain the event reference. Query results remain subject to the selected filters and account access. A status update can change whether a record belongs in the current list.</p><h2>Use Cases</h2><p>Review recent alarms, investigate a historical interval, isolate an event type, or track pending, confirmed, closed, and false-alarm records. When the same type recurs, compare footage and notes to distinguish an ongoing risk from a rule issue or environmental interference.</p><p data-editorial-revision=\"enterprise-v1.5\">For acceptance, use a known test event and verify that it can be found by the supported fields and relevant time range. Open View and compare the event identity and metadata with the test. Repeat the search after a permitted status change using a scope that includes the saved outcome. Record the criteria and result so another reviewer can reproduce the check.</p><h2>Search Procedure</h2><ol><li>Enter a known keyword or open the filter panel to set the date and time range.</li><li>Add the required event type or status, then apply the filters.</li><li>Verify the camera, location, and timestamp. Browse additional pages and open relevant records.</li><li>If no results appear, broaden one criterion at a time. Check the time zone, camera permissions, and interval before concluding that no event exists.</li><li>After investigation, save the response. Verify the updated status and notes in the list.</li></ol><p data-editorial-revision=\"enterprise-v1.5\">Begin with the most reliable reference available, such as a camera, location, or approximate time. Add type and state filters only after establishing that the relevant period is included. When a search returns no records, remove one restriction at a time and keep track of the conditions used. Confirm the account’s camera scope before escalating an apparently missing event.</p><h2>Interface Example</h2><img src=\"/media/polytron-one/uploads/en/20260702120252-1urmv5-notification.png\" alt=\"notification\" title=\"notification\">",
